  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different boron-cluster anions interact with a gold surface coated either with organic species or a coordination polymer, and how these interactions change the surface structure. Across the panels, pink cluster ions approach the interface and either bind directly to exposed carboxylate-rich organic layers or become incorporated into a more crowded interfacial film. Insets show close-up molecular models of the key contact points between the clusters and nearby organic groups.\n\nA central comparison is between more weakly interacting clusters, which seem simply to adsorb at the surface, and functionalized clusters containing groups such as N, S(CN), SH, CN, or phosphonium-substituted aryl units, which appear to form more specific associations with the surrounding organic matrix. Several panels depict exchange or competition between two anionic species, suggesting that one cluster can replace another at the interface. One case also shows thermal treatment at 200 °C with loss of N2, leading to a different cluster-derived surface assembly.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that cluster composition and substituents strongly control adsorption, exchange, and organization on Au-supported films, producing distinct interfacial architectures.",
